<section xml:id="section-integers">
  <title>The Integers</title>
  <p>
    Abstract algebra is going to take the familiar number sets and
    abstract them, pulling their properties away from the numbers
    themselves seeing what happens with the general ideas. To do
    this, I need to set a solid foundation of numbers. All the
    number sets will be useful in this course, but the integers are
    have a special central place. It's pretty valuable to review
    properties of the integers. A lot of this is review is likely of
    ideas from many years ago: ideas that you originally may have
    learned somewhere in elementary school. That said, my definitions
    will differ a bit from what you may have learned so long ago; I do
    so in order to properly setup important definitions later in the
    course. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I'll start with one very important property of the integers,
    which I won't prove. (What a proof even constitute here is a bit
    tricky to say: this is almost by definition, depending on your
    perpsective.) 
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The relation <m>a \leq b</m> is a total order on <m>\ZZ</m>.
        Similarly, the relation <m>a \lt b</m> is a strict total
        order on <m>\ZZ</m>.  
      </p>
    </statement>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    Then I can move on to the arithmetic of the integers. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Let <m>a,b,n\in \ZZ</m>. If <m>n = ab</m>, then <m>a</m>
            and <m>b</m> are called <term>factors</term> of
            <m>n</m>. The number <m>n</m> is called a
            <term>multiple</term> of <m>a</m> and likewise a multiple
            of <m>b</m>. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The numbers <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m> in <m>\ZZ</m> are
            called <term>units</term>.
          </li>
          <li>
            A non-zero, non-unit integer <m>n</m> is called <term>
            prime</term> if, in any integer equation <m>n = ab</m>,
            one of <m>a</m> or <m>b</m> is a unit. Equivalently,
            <m>n</m> has no integer factors other than itself,
            excluding units and multiplication by units (meaning
            the factors are <m>n</m>, <m>-n</m>, <m>1</m> and
            <m>-1</m>.)
          </li>
          <li>
            A non-zero, non-unit integer <m>n</m> is called
            <term>composite</term> if it is not prime. This means that
            there is a factorization <m>n = ab</m> where neither
            <m>a</m> nor <m>b</m> is a unit. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The <term>greatest common factor</term> of two integers
            <m>a</m> amd <m>b</m> is, unsurprisingly, the largest
            positive integer which is a factor of both. 
          </li>
          <li>
            The <term>least common multiple</term> of two integers
            <m>a</m> amd <m>b</m> is, again unsurprisingly, the
            smallest positive integer which is a multiple of both. 
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Now let me state, as theorems, two of the most important
    properties of the integers. I don't prove these here, but
    versions of the proofs of this will show up later when we get
    into similar concpets in ring theory.  
  </p>
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-fundamental-arithmetic">
    <title>Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>n \in \ZZ</m> be non-zero and not a unit. Then there
        exists a list of positive prime integers <m>p_1, p_2, \ldots
        p_k</m> and a unit <m>u</m> such that this equation holds.
        <md>
          n = up_1p_2 \ldots p_k
        </md>  
        This list is called the <term>prime factors</term> of
        <m>n</m>. The primes in the list are not necessarily distinct;
        indeed, the number of times any prime shows up in the list
        is called the <term>multiplicity</term> of that prime factor.
        The list of prime factors is unique up to reordering and up
        to multiplication by units. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <theorem xml:id="theorem-division-algorithm">
    <title>Division Algorithm</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> be integers with <m>b > 0</m>.
        Then there exists unique integers <m>q</m> and <m>r</m> with
        two properties.
        <ul>
          <li>
            <m>a = bq + r</m>
          </li>
          <li>
            <m>0 \leq r \lt b</m> 
          </li>
        </ul>
        The number <m>q</m> is called the <term>quotient</term> of
        <m>a</m> divided by <m>b</m>, and the number <m>r</m> is
        called the <term>remainder</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
  <p>
    The division algorithm just states that division with remainder
    always works for the integers. This is obvious from elementary
    school arithmetic, but it is still one of the central facts
    about the structure of the integers, and one which becomes very
    interesting when we abstract the integers. 
  </p>
  <p>
    The division algorithm is very useful. In particular, I can use
    it to calculate greatest common divisors.  
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The exists a process to calculate greatest common divisors
        called the <term>Euclidean algorithm</term>. Start with the
        two integers <m>a_1</m> and <m>b_1</m>. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Use the division algorithm to calculate the quotient and
        remainder <m>q_1</m> and <m>r_1</m>. That gives this
        equation
        <md>
          a_1 = q_1 b_1 + r_1 
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Then set <m>a_2 = b_1</m> and <m>b_2 = r_1</m>. Then
        repeat, calculating <m>q_2</m> and <m>r_2</m>. 
        <md>
          a_2 = q_2 b_2 + r_2 
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Then keep repeating. Each time this repeats, the next
        <m>b_i</m> is the remainder of the previous step and the
        next <m>a_i</m> is the <m>b_{i-1}</m> of the previous
        step. 
      </p>
      <p>
        As a remainder, each <m>b_i</m> satisfies <m>b_i = r_{i-1}
        \lt b_{i-1}</m>.  The sequence of <m>b_i</m> is a sequence of
        positive decreasing numbers. It eventually must reach  0. If the
        last step has index <m>k</m>, it looks like this:
        <md>
          a_k = q_k b_k + 0 
        </md>
        From this, you can prove that <m>b_k</m> is actually the
        greatest common disivor. Just iterating the division algorith
        accomplishes this fact. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="definition-set-quotient"/>, I defined the set
    quotient by an equivalence relation. Let me apply this now to
    the integers for the most important example.  
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>a, b, n \in \ZZ</m>. If <m>a-b</m> is a multiple of
        <m>n</m>, then we say that <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are
        <term>congruence modulo </term> <m>n</m>. This is written as
        follows. 
        <md>
          a \equiv b \mod n
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    For a fixed <m>n</m>, this congruence is an equivalence relation on
    <m>\ZZ</m>. (Feel free to check the definition if you wish.). By
    <xref ref="proposition-equivalence-relation-partition" />, I know
    that the equivalence relation partitions <m>\ZZ</m> into
    equivalence classes. Since elements in each equivalence class
    differ by multiples of <m>n</m>, I can write these classes as
    follows.
    <md>
      \{ \bar{0}, \bar{1}, \bar{2}, \ldots, \overline{n-1} \}
    </md>
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="definition-integers-mod">
    <statement>
      <p>
        These equivalence classes form a set with the notation
        <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m>. This set is called the integers modulo
        <m>n</m> (or just mod <m>n</m>). 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    The integers mod <m>n</m> is a pretty important example for
    many parts of the course. It inherits addition and multiplication
    from the integers (again, feel free to check that these
    operations are well-defined <mdash/> an excellent example of the
    use of that tricky term as well.) 
  </p>
</section>
